python\n# Explanation why the column SCATTER_RATIO is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['SCATTER_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: SCATTER_RATIO has a fairly large standard deviation (33.9061) but does not intuitively align with clear physical interpretations specifically related to the vehicle classification task. Derived interaction features like CIRCULARITY_X_COMPACTNESS may better capture similar characteristics with direct relevance.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a very low variability (std = 4.4612) and is narrow in range, and its effects might already be captured through derived features such as CIRCULARITY_X_COMPACTNESS and LOG_ELONGATEDNESS.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Despite its moderate standard deviation (9.0532), this feature has a narrow, non-informative range ([0, 40]), and is unlikely to add meaningful discriminative power for vehicle classification.\nend", "python\n# 1. RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY\n# Feature: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY.\ndf['RATIO_COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['CIRCULARITY']\n# Usefulness: This ratio measures how the compactness of the shape relates to its circularity. Since vehicles have silhouettes with differing levels of compactness and circularity (e.g., a BUS may have a more compact yet less circular profile than an OPEL), this feature could help differentiate classes.\nend\n\npython\n# 2. INTERACTION_SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS\n# Feature: Product of SCATTER_RATIO and ELONGATEDNESS.\ndf['INTERACTION_SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] * df['ELONGATEDNESS']\n# Usefulness: The combination of these two features captures how the overall elongation of a silhouette relates to its scatter ratio, which may vary significantly across ve